  - [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT = "https://api.example.com/login"[39m is the only config value today, and it's a full URL including the
    [38;5;153m/login[39m path.
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are a separate CommonJS Node entry point, unrelated to the webapp.

  [1mThe fork — when the environment gets chosen.[0m Everything else (file shape, how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads it, whether you need a
  build step) follows from this one answer:

  - [1mRuntime detection[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m holds a table of all environments and picks one by [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[39m. Buys:
    one file ships everywhere, switching envs is zero-deploy-work; you can point localhost at staging by editing one
    line. Costs: every environment's URLs are visible in the shipped source. Rules out nothing later, and it's cheap to
    change.
  - [1mDeploy-time swap[0m — one [38;5;153msettings.js[39m per environment, the deploy copies the right one into place. Buys: only the
    active environment's URL ships. Costs: a real deploy step you don't have yet, and [38;5;153msettings.js[39m becomes a build
    artifact rather than source. This one is the expensive one to back out of, because it puts a requirement on your
    deploy pipeline.
  - [1mInjected global[0m — the server writes [38;5;153mwindow.APP_CONFIG[39m into [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, [38;5;153msettings.js[39m reads it with a fallback. Buys:
    config without a rebuild. Costs: you have no server here — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is static — so this means adding one.

  My recommendation is [1mruntime detection by hostname[0m: you have no build step and no server, the endpoint is a public API
  URL rather than a secret, and if you later add a pipeline you can swap to deploy-time without touching [38;5;153mapp.js[39m at all
  — the settings module's interface stays the same either way.
